What every target emits for a timer, and where it finds one.

`timer` is an ordinary component, and every target overrides it with what it
schedules with. Three do it with an `effect` bracketing a pair of host natives:
html over setInterval/clearInterval, fyne over time.AfterFunc, gtk4 over
gtk4rt. Such a pair already is a lifetime with a thing to release, which is
what an effect brackets.

fyne's is the one whose pair is a *one-shot*: `AfterFunc` fires once, so the
schedule re-arms itself from inside the closure it posts to the drawing
thread, and the `gen`/`mine` pair below is what stops a post that was already
queued when the effect re-keyed from arming a timer the unmount can no longer
see. It cannot be asserted by running: fyne's test driver dispatches `fyne.Do`
inline on the calling goroutine, so the queued window does not exist there.

fyne consumes a `*time.Ticker` on a goroutine of its own, and `go.select` is
what ends it. `Ticker.Stop` does not close `C`, so a receive from it alone
parks for the life of the process; `done` is the second arm, closed rather than
sent on, because a closed channel is always ready and the arm is taken on the
very next pass. **The two have to be a select and not a sequence** -- two
receives in a row would take the tick before ever looking at `done` -- and that
is the one thing only a golden can show, which is what the emitted `select {`
below is here for.

`sngl:language/go` grew `go.chan[T]` for this: `C` is a `<-chan Time` field, and
without a channel type there was nothing to declare it as. Direction is not
spelled -- a receive-only channel is reachable because nothing writes a Go type
for an expression that is only selected on.

Three still carry a primitive of their own, which stands in the tree where it
was written and which codegen reads the schedule off; two of those are because
an effect's `@mount` hands over a
*closure* and a closure is only a schedule where the host may run it against
live state: bubbletea is Elm, so a tick has to arrive as a message and its body
has to be a case Update runs, which is a period and a body together rather than
a closure; and android's LaunchedEffect is the schedule rather than something a
schedule is built from. none is the third and is a different case -- the
interpreter honors `effect` itself and owns the clock, so an override would
bracket correctly and schedule nothing.

So this asserts three things at once: that a timer is emitted at all, that a
timer written in a child component is emitted too, and that its gate reaches
the backend as an expression.

On the three effect targets the gate is the bracket's *position*: `if enabled`
is what makes toggling it a mount and an unmount, and `on=interval` is the key,
so a schedule is re-armed when its period changes and left alone when it does
not. Read the `__effect0_down` bodies below -- the desired list is empty or
holds one key, and that is the whole of the gate.

Both were broken before, in opposite directions and on different targets:

  - gtk4 emitted no timer whatsoever. The analysis read a list the checker had
    hoisted every timer onto and this platform never looked at it, so a program
    with a timer compiled clean and never ticked.
  - android reduced the gate to a bare variable name, so `enabled=true` -- which
    names no variable -- came out as `LaunchedEffect() { while () {`. That is
    not Kotlin. No golden covered an android timer to say so.

And the child component's timer was emitted by nobody: `AnalyzeCommon` collected
`pkg.Timers` plus `main`'s, and a timer anywhere else was checked, type-correct
and inert.

deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `while \(\)` -- a gate is an expression, not a variable name
deny out/kotlin/android/MainScreen.kt `LaunchedEffect\(\)` -- likewise: LaunchedEffect with no key does not compile
deny * `timer\(interval` -- the primitive leaves the tree; no target renders one
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^\t+<-ticker\.C$` -- a bare receive outside a select is a sequence, and the tick would be taken before `done` was ever looked at
deny out/go/fyne/model.go `if !m.live` -- fyne re-arms from inside the post, so a stale closure must test the generation it was armed in: a re-key is an unmount and a mount back to back, so a flag saying only that something is armed is true again by the time the stale post runs, and it would Reset the timer that mount armed back to the period it captured

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

component ticker(label string) node {
    var beats = 0

    // A literal gate: the shape that produced android's `while ()`.
    timer(interval=250ms, enabled=true, @tick {
        beats += 1
    })

    text(value="{label} {beats}")
}

component main node {
    var (
        seconds = 0
        running = true
    )

    // A gate that is a state read, which is the only shape the old analysis
    // could carry.
    timer(interval=1000ms, enabled=running, @tick {
        seconds += 1
    })

    ticker(label="beats")
    text(value=string(seconds))
}

window {
    main
}
